Arrays2_BoundedMaximum
Success rate: 32.95%
Given an integer array nums and two integers left and right, return the number of contiguous non-empty subarrays such that the value of the maximum array element in that subarray is in the range [left, right].

Input Format:
The first line contains an integer n (length of the array nums).
The second line contains n space-separated integers representing the elements of the array nums.
The third line contains two space-separated integers left and right.

Output Format:
A single integer representing the number of contiguous non-empty subarrays where the value of the maximum 
array element in that subarray is in the range [left, right].

Constraints:
1 <= nums.length <= 105
0 <= nums[i] <= 109
0 <= left <= right <= 109

Sample test cases
Input
5
2 9 2 5 6
2 8
Expected output
7
Input
5
2 1 4 3 5
2 4
Expected output
9

----------------------------------------------------
T.C: O(N^2), S.C: O(1)

import java.util.Scanner;
public class Main {
    static void boundedMaximum(int[] nums, int left, int right){
		int n = nums.length;
		int count = 0;
		for(int i=0;i<n;i++){
        int currentMax = Integer.MIN_VALUE;
		for(int j=i;j<n;j++){
        currentMax = Math.max(currentMax,nums[j]);

        if(currentMax>=left && currentMax<=right){
			  count++;
			 }
		 }
  }
	 System.out.println(count); 
	}


